    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p18-q4-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta=\\alpha+\\alpha=2\\alpha$, so $\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}$.",
          "worked": "Since $AB=BD$, triangle $ABD$ is isosceles, so $\\angle ADB=\\angle BAD=\\alpha$. By the exterior angle theorem,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\theta&=\\angle BAD+\\angle ADB\\\\\\theta&=\\alpha+\\alpha\\\\\\theta&=2\\alpha.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 39
      },
      {
        "id": "p18-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that $\\tan\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}=\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{1+\\cos\\theta}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\tan\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}=\\tan\\alpha=\\dfrac{CD}{AB+BC}=\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{1+\\cos\\theta}$.",
          "worked": "In right-angled triangle $BCD$, $BD=1$, so $CD=\\sin\\theta$ and $BC=\\cos\\theta$. Using part (a) and triangle $ACD$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan\\frac{\\theta}{2}&=\\tan\\alpha\\\\\\tan\\frac{\\theta}{2}&=\\frac{CD}{AC}\\\\\\tan\\frac{\\theta}{2}&=\\frac{CD}{AB+BC}\\\\\\tan\\frac{\\theta}{2}&=\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{1+\\cos\\theta}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p18-q4-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Use the above formula to find the exact value of $\\tan22.5^\\circ$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt2-1$.\n\nSubstitute $\\theta=45^\\circ$ into the identity, then rationalise.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\tan22.5^\\circ&=\\frac{\\sin45^\\circ}{1+\\cos45^\\circ}\\\\\\tan22.5^\\circ&=\\frac{1/\\sqrt2}{1+1/\\sqrt2}\\\\\\tan22.5^\\circ&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2+1}\\\\\\tan22.5^\\circ&=\\frac{\\sqrt2-1}{(\\sqrt2+1)(\\sqrt2-1)}\\\\\\tan22.5^\\circ&=\\sqrt2-1.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p18-q4-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Use this to determine the area of a regular octagon of side length $1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2+2\\sqrt2$ square units.\n\nDivide the octagon into eight congruent triangles and use $\\tan22.5^\\circ$ to find their perpendicular height.",
          "worked": "Join the centre to each vertex and bisect one of the eight congruent triangles. Its central half-angle is $22.5^\\circ$, its half-base is $\\tfrac12$, and its perpendicular height is $h$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan22.5^\\circ&=\\frac{1/2}{h}\\\\h&=\\frac{1}{2\\tan22.5^\\circ}\\\\h&=\\frac{1}{2(\\sqrt2-1)}\\\\h&=\\frac{\\sqrt2+1}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=8\\times\\frac12\\times1\\times h\\\\A&=4\\times\\frac{\\sqrt2+1}{2}\\\\A&=2+2\\sqrt2\\text{ square units}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      }
    ]
  },
